# Architecture invariants

Preserve unless an approved ADR/SoT change says otherwise:

- Gazebo is a simulation plant, not the production factory or the MES.
- `Equipment` / `GenericEquipment` is an open-ended technical asset model. Conveyor, robot, pump, oven, mixer are **examples**, not a closed catalog. Do not add a C++ class per machine type without a strong, approved reason.
- Equipment must not depend on Gazebo, open62541, Modbus/HTTP/MQTT/CIP/PROFINET libraries, or vendor SDKs.
- Industrial adapters are **protocol-oriented** (`OpcUaIndustrialAdapter`, `ModbusIndustrialAdapter`, `RestIndustrialAdapter`, `MqttIndustrialAdapter`, mock; planned EtherNet/IP/PROFINET). Never `PumpOpcUaAdapter` / `RobotOpcUaAdapter`.
- One `OpcUaIndustrialAdapter` = one `UA_Client` = one OPC UA endpoint. One `ModbusIndustrialAdapter` = one TCP session. One `RestIndustrialAdapter` = one HTTP origin. One `MqttIndustrialAdapter` = one MQTT broker. Many sources = many adapter instances.
- Adding industrial endpoints later must be configuration/onboarding (Phase 7), not a new C++ adapter class per PLC and not a Phase 6 adapter manager.
- MES owns resource management, hierarchy, readiness, scheduling, materials, quality, genealogy, OEE/analytics. Equipment state ≠ MES availability. Capability ≠ availability.
- MES must not depend on Gazebo or protocol/vendor APIs. Adapters must not own MES scheduling.
- SCADA is visualization/operational control, not MES. Auth/RBAC is Phase 9. ERP/PLM/QMS/CMMS and advanced what-if are Phase 11.
- Keep dependency direction: Gazebo → sim integration; Equipment → generic model; Adapter → protocol; MES → production/resources; SCADA → HMI. No reverse leaks.
